literal module, its `pilot.py` import, inherited canonical, and the LUB
proof/source/verifier/canonical inputs. [SHA256SUMS](SHA256SUMS) is
package-directory-relative and covers every package file except itself.
See [ARTIFACT_AUDIT.md](ARTIFACT_AUDIT.md) for actual closure checks.

Only this `HVD_PROOF_WORK/` directory was edited for this task.
Central indexes, the inherited atlas, historical packages and Git were
not changed. Root may later record the author-side HOLD disposition;
this package does not itself perform that central mutation.
# Existing HVD — bounded proof/source work contract

2026-09-06 UTC. Author: `batch197_fifth_scout`.
Only this directory is writable. Earlier scouts, the root visibility
pilot and its canonical, central state and Git are read-only here.

For $n\geq1$, the carrier is $\{0,\ldots,n-1\}^n$.
The horizontal visibility graph has an edge $i<j$ exactly when
$x_k<\min(x_i,x_j)$ for every $i<k<j$. The autonomous update is
the ordered **undirected** degree sequence. All repeated entries and
all endpoint states are included. The literal source is
[visibility_local_pilot.py](../visibility_local_pilot.py); original
HVD census rows use $n=1,\ldots,6$ only in
[VISIBILITY_LOCAL_CANONICAL.jsonl](../VISIBILITY_LOCAL_CANONICAL.jsonl).

Do not regenerate or enlarge the six-rule atlas. Further execution
must audit a stated proof or a named counterexample, within the
original boxes unless it is a single explicitly derived/source-given
sentinel. No new full box is authorized by such a sentinel.

Admission needs a proved all-length temporal/core statement or a
meaningful sharp clock, together with a materially independent,
evaluated inverse/extremal residual after complete static adapters.
Canonical HVG recognition, degree-sequence reconstruction, max/merge
trees, ordinary order-polynomial counting and a finite atlas alone
receive no separate value credit. A partial result may close HOLD/KILL.

The three initially requested primary sources are arXiv:1605.05222,
2111.02723 and 1704.06467. Actual body access must be distinguished
from metadata; source reconstruction assumptions do not transfer to
degree feedback without checking repeated values and endpoints.
No external manuscript upload, specialist contact or release.

## Explicit rejected proof routes and planned sentinels

The parent supplied the source
$(0,2,1,1,0,2)$, whose next two states are
$(1,3,2,3,2,3)$ and $(1,3,2,4,2,2)$.
An edge can be created, and the square norm increases from 36 to 38.
Neither edge containment nor a decreasing square norm is assumed.

The primary 2021 paper, Figure 6, gives two length-seven data
sequences with different HVGs but the same ordered degree sequence:
$(3,2,2,1,2,2,3)$ and $(2,1,2,2,2,1,2)$.
Checking these two specified examples is not a length-seven atlas.

A further deductively constructed length-nine source is
$(0,2,1,2,2,2,1,2,0)$. Its graph is the Hamiltonian path plus
$(1,3)$ and $(5,7)$ in zero-based indices; its degree sequence
$(1,3,2,3,2,3,2,3,1)$ additionally makes $(3,5)$ visible.
Thus the edge count increases from 10 to 11. This single named
sentinel tests the proposed edge-count potential, not a new full box.

Current partial proof directions are endpoint erosion, permanence
of interior degree two, fixed low-degree subfamilies and the exact
weakly-unimodal path-target fibre. They do not yet imply a full
recurrent atlas or a maximum-fibre theorem.
